{"t":"2026-09-26T06:27:18.565Z","mode":"turns","direction":"daemon->client","payload":{"id":1,"result":{"data":[{"id":"01a0dc63-5d19-7ad3-a6c3-1d370e082f10","items":[{"type":"userMessage","id":"01a0dc63-5e01-7d31-ae2b-35d2848d1703","clientId":"b60f3ff8-3842-40bd-b60c-8ff76d3e3a81","content":[{"type":"text","text":"G2 spike queued message (sent via thread/queue/add while the previous turn was still running): after finishing, reply with exactly the words OAC G2 QUEUED.","text_elements":[]}]},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"msg_01d7d14f18812392016ab76538969c87d191f5d193cc264acc","text":"OAC G2 QUEUED","phase":"final_answer","memoryCitation":null,"delivery":null,"questions":null}],"itemsView":"full","status":"completed","error":null,"startedAt":1790403894,"completedAt":1790403896,"durationMs":1751},{"id":"01a0dc63-229a-79a3-aeae-6eec74b158ca","items":[{"type":"userMessage","id":"01a0dc63-2329-7f42-84ad-d7bfceeb2c6b","clientId":null,"content":[{"type":"text","text":"G2 spike busy-turn test: write a numbered list of 40 distinct one-sentence facts about lighthouses. Do not use any tools.","text_elements":[]}]},{"type":"reasoning","id":"rs_01d7d14f18812392016ab76529389487d1953a4daac198eb91","summary":[],"content":[]},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"msg_01d7d14f18812392016ab76529afa487d1b8749f7efff55877","text":"1. Lighthouses help ships identify coastlines and hazards.\n2. The light from a lighthouse is called a beacon.\n3. Many lighthouses stand on rocky headlands.\n4. Some lighthouses are built on reefs far from shore.\n5. Lighthouse lenses focus light into powerful beams.\n6. Fresnel lenses use ridged glass to gather and direct light.\n7. Augustin-Jean Fresnel developed the lens design in the 19th century.\n8. Lighthouse beams often rotate to create a recognizable pattern.\n9. Different lighthouses use different flash patterns.\n10. Mariners can identify some lighthouses by their light patterns.\n11. Lighthouse towers are often painted in contrasting colors.\n12. Daymarks are the shapes and colors used to identify towers in daylight.\n13. Some lighthouses use sound signals in fog.\n14. Fog signals can include horns or bells.\n15. Keepers once lived in homes attached to or near many lighthouses.\n16. Lighthouse keepers maintained lamps and equipment.\n17. Some isolated lighthouses required keepers to live there for weeks at a time.\n18. Many lighthouses are now automated.\n19. Solar panels power some modern lighthouse beacons.\n20. Lighthouses can be made from stone, brick, iron, or concrete.\n21. The tallest lighthouses can rise more than 100 meters.\n22. The oldest known lighthouse of antiquity was the Pharos of Alexandria.\n23. The Pharos stood on an island near the Egyptian city of Alexandria.\n24. The Tower of Hercules in Spain is an ancient lighthouse still in use.\n25. Lighthouses are sometimes called light stations when they include other buildings.\n26. A lighthouse’s color and flash pattern are recorded on nautical charts.\n27. Some lighthouses are built on stilts above the water.\n28. Offshore lighthouses must withstand waves, wind, and salt spray.\n29. Lighthouse windows are often placed high in the tower around the lantern room.\n30. The lantern room houses the light source and often its lens.\n31. Before electric lamps, many lighthouses used oil or gas lamps.\n32. Some historic lighthouse lenses are displayed in museums.\n33. Lighthouses can help aircraft and land travelers as well as ships.\n34. A lighthouse’s height affects how far its light can be seen over the horizon.\n35. Atmospheric conditions can change how far a lighthouse beam is visible.\n36. Some lighthouses are accessible to visitors through guided tours.\n37. Lighthouse foundations are designed to resist erosion and strong waves.\n38. A leading light is one of a pair of lights used to guide ships along a channel.\n39. Lighthouse services are operated by maritime authorities in many countries.\n40. Lighthouses have become common subjects in paintings, photographs, and stories.","phase":"final_answer","memoryCitation":null,"delivery":null,"questions":null}],"itemsView":"full","status":"completed","error":null,"startedAt":1790403879,"completedAt":1790403894,"durationMs":14946},{"id":"01a0dc63-00d3-78a3-bdbd-2f0666ca8686","items":[{"type":"userMessage","id":"01a0dc63-016f-70b0-9ab9-1bb09fd240f5","clientId":null,"content":[{"type":"text","text":"G2 re-run on 0.157.1 (from a second daemon client, not typed in this TUI): reply with exactly the words OAC G2 RERUN RECEIVED.","text_elements":[]}]},{"type":"reasoning","id":"rs_01d7d14f18812392016ab76520bc8087d1a573b8ba9d32bc0f","summary":[],"content":[]},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"msg_01d7d14f18812392016ab76521036087d186e269d641407699","text":"OAC G2 RERUN RECEIVED","phase":"final_answer","memoryCitation":null,"delivery":null,"questions":null}],"itemsView":"full","status":"completed","error":null,"startedAt":1790403870,"completedAt":1790403873,"durationMs":2067},{"id":"01a0dc61-b06d-7630-a64e-72d77c3c2cd9","items":[{"type":"userMessage","id":"01a0dc61-b217-7f62-9def-51b70f9545f1","clientId":"f532e073-2393-4fdb-926c-1b4d504e043e","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Greet me in a single word.","text_elements":[]}]},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"msg_01d7d14f18812392016ab764cb059487d185283335e5389903","text":"Hello","phase":"final_answer","memoryCitation":null,"delivery":null,"questions":null}],"itemsView":"full","status":"completed","error":null,"startedAt":1790403784,"completedAt":1790403786,"durationMs":2081}],"nextCursor":null,"backwardsCursor":"{\"requestedThreadId\":\"01a0dc61-364b-7ed3-b686-b04e07eecaf2\",\"rolloutOrdinal\":37,\"includeAnchor\":true,\"scope\":{\"kind\":\"turns\"}}"}}}
